        {
          "secNumber": "5.6",
          "heading": "The Wald-Wolfowitz Optimality Theorem and Truncated Sequential Designs",
          "content": "Why did Abraham Wald select constant horizontal boundaries $(b, a)$ for the cumulative log-likelihood ratio $S_m$ rather than curved or time-varying boundaries $(b_m, a_m)$? In 1948, Abraham Wald and Jacob Wolfowitz proved one of the deepest theorems in mathematical statistics: among **all** sequential and fixed-sample tests whose error probabilities do not exceed those of the SPRT, the SPRT simultaneously minimizes the expected sample size under **both** $H_0$ and $H_1$!\n\n### Theorem 5.5 (Wald-Wolfowitz Optimality Theorem for the SPRT)\nLet $\\mathcal{T}^*$ be a Sequential Probability Ratio Test with stopping time $N^*$ and terminal decision $\\delta^*$ having error probabilities $\\alpha(\\mathcal{T}^*) = \\alpha$ and $\\beta(\\mathcal{T}^*) = \\beta$. Let $\\mathcal{T} = (N, \\delta)$ be any other sequential or fixed-sample test such that:\n\n$$\\alpha(\\mathcal{T}) \\le \\alpha \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\beta(\\mathcal{T}) \\le \\beta$$\n\nThen the expected sample size of $\\mathcal{T}^*$ is simultaneous smaller than or equal to that of $\\mathcal{T}$ under both hypotheses:\n\n$$\\mathbb{E}_{\\theta_0}[N^*] \\le \\mathbb{E}_{\\theta_0}[N] \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\mathbb{E}_{\\theta_1}[N^*] \\le \\mathbb{E}_{\\theta_1}[N]$$\n\n#### Sketch of Proof via Bayesian Sequential Decision Theory and Kullback-Leibler Divergence:\nConsider a Bayesian sequential decision problem where $\\theta \\in \\{\\theta_0, \\theta_1\\}$ is assigned prior probability $\\pi = \\mathbb{P}(\\theta = \\theta_1) \\in (0, 1)$, let $c > 0$ be the cost per observation, and let $w_0, w_1 > 0$ be the terminal losses for Type I and Type II errors. The total Bayes risk of any test $\\mathcal{T} = (N, \\delta)$ is:\n\n$$R(\\pi, \\mathcal{T}) = (1 - \\pi)\\left( w_0 \\alpha(\\mathcal{T}) + c \\mathbb{E}_{\\theta_0}[N] \\right) + \\pi\\left( w_1 \\beta(\\mathcal{T}) + c \\mathbb{E}_{\\theta_1}[N] \\right)$$\n\nBy backward induction and Bellman's optimality equation for Markov decision processes, since the posterior probability $\\pi_m = \\mathbb{P}(\\theta = \\theta_1 \\mid X_1, \\dots, X_m)$ is a monotone function of the likelihood ratio $\\Lambda_m$ via Bayes' rule:\n\n$$\\frac{\\pi_m}{1 - \\pi_m} = \\frac{\\pi}{1 - \\pi} \\Lambda_m$$\n\nand the future observation dynamics are time-homogeneous, the optimal Bayes continuation region in terms of $\\pi_m$ is a time-invariant open interval $(\\pi_L, \\pi_U)$, which transforms via Bayes' rule into a time-invariant interval $B < \\Lambda_m < A$, i.e., an SPRT! By varying the prior $\\pi$ and loss weights $(w_0, w_1)$ to match the given boundaries $(B, A)$, if any competing test $\\mathcal{T}$ had $\\alpha(\\mathcal{T}) \\le \\alpha(\\mathcal{T}^*)$, $\\beta(\\mathcal{T}) \\le \\beta(\\mathcal{T}^*)$, and strictly smaller $\\mathbb{E}_{\\theta_0}[N] < \\mathbb{E}_{\\theta_0}[N^*]$ (or $\\mathbb{E}_{\\theta_1}[N] < \\mathbb{E}_{\\theta_1}[N^*]$), it would achieve a strictly smaller Bayes risk than the Bayes-optimal SPRT $\\mathcal{T}^*$, which is a contradiction.\n\nWe can also derive a universal **Information-Theoretic Lower Bound** on $\\mathbb{E}_{\\theta_0}[N]$ and $\\mathbb{E}_{\\theta_1}[N]$ directly from Wald's Equation and the data-processing inequality for Kullback-Leibler divergence. Note that $\\mathbb{E}_{\\theta_0}[Z_1] = -D_{\\text{KL}}(f_0 \\parallel f_1)$. Applying Jensen's inequality conditionally on the terminal decision $\\delta_N \\in \\{0, 1\\}$ to $\\mathbb{E}_{\\theta_0}[S_N] = \\mathbb{E}_{\\theta_0}[\\ln \\Lambda_N]$ yields:\n\n$$-\\mathbb{E}_{\\theta_0}[S_N] = \\mathbb{E}_{\\theta_0}[N] D_{\\text{KL}}(f_0 \\parallel f_1) \\ge (1 - \\alpha) \\ln\\left(\\frac{1 - \\alpha}{\\beta}\\right) + \\alpha \\ln\\left(\\frac{\\alpha}{1 - \\beta}\\right) = \\text{kl}(1 - \\alpha \\parallel \\beta)$$\n\nThus, for **any** test $\\mathcal{T} = (N, \\delta)$ with error probabilities $(\\alpha, \\beta)$:\n\n$$\\mathbb{E}_{\\theta_0}[N] \\ge \\frac{(1 - \\alpha) \\ln\\left(\\frac{1 - \\alpha}{\\beta}\\right) + \\alpha \\ln\\left(\\frac{\\alpha}{1 - \\beta}\\right)}{D_{\\text{KL}}(f_0 \\parallel f_1)}, \\qquad \\mathbb{E}_{\\theta_1}[N] \\ge \\frac{(1 - \\beta) \\ln\\left(\\frac{1 - \\beta}{\\alpha}\\right) + \\beta \\ln\\left(\\frac{\\beta}{1 - \\alpha}\\right)}{D_{\\text{KL}}(f_1 \\parallel f_0)}$$\n\nComparing the numerator of this universal lower bound with Wald's ASN formula at $\\theta_0$ and $\\theta_1$ using $\\ln A \\approx \\ln\\left(\\frac{1-\\beta}{\\alpha}\\right)$ and $\\ln B \\approx \\ln\\left(\\frac{\\beta}{1-\\alpha}\\right)$, we see that **Wald's SPRT attains the exact information-theoretic lower bound** up to the negligible boundary overshoot!\n\nIn clinical trials and industrial applications, one practical concern with the open-ended SPRT is that if the true parameter happens to lie near the indifference point $\\theta^*$, the sample size $N$, though finite with probability one, has a positively skewed distribution and may occasionally exceed the fixed-sample size $n_{\\text{fixed}}$. Furthermore, budgeting and hospital logistics require a hard upper bound $N_{\\max}$ on the number of patients enrolled. This motivates **Truncated Sequential Designs** and **Group Sequential Designs**:\n- **Wald's Truncated SPRT**: If the SPRT has not terminated by a pre-specified maximum stage $N_{\\max}$, sampling is forcibly stopped at $N_{\\max}$, and $H_0$ is accepted if $S_{N_{\\max}} \\le 0$ and rejected if $S_{N_{\\max}} > 0$.\n- **Group Sequential Clinical Trials (Pocock, O'Brien-Fleming, and Lan-DeMets $\\alpha$-Spending Boundaries)**: Rather than monitoring after every single patient, data are analyzed at $K$ interim looks (e.g., $K = 4$ or $5$ stages of $m$ patients each). Pocock (1977) proposed constant standardized $Z$-thresholds across all looks, whereas O'Brien and Fleming (1979) proposed conservative early thresholds $c_k = c_{\\text{OBF}} \\sqrt{K/k}$ that make early stopping very stringent unless the treatment effect is dramatic, while preserving a final critical value $c_K$ nearly identical to the fixed-sample test.\n\n---",
          "simulations": []
        }
